{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "id": "ms-rag-grounded-knowledge-assistant-v1",
  "title": "Grounded knowledge assistant with retrieval-augmented generation",
  "subtitle": "A standard RAG pattern pairs an application orchestrator with indexed source material so answers can be grounded in retrieved passages. Synthetic Azure reference; no deployment is implied.",
  "description": "### Problem\nUsers need answers based on private or domain-specific documents that a general language model might not know. A fixed retrieval pipeline is a useful starting point for single-index question answering.\n\n### Flow and Azure mapping\nAn editor or source system supplies documents; an ingestion pipeline chunks and enriches them, creates embeddings, and writes an Azure AI Search index. A user sends a question through an application API; an orchestrator retrieves relevant passages, builds a grounded prompt, calls a selected model endpoint, and returns a response with citations. Keep ingestion and query paths distinct in the diagram.\n\n### Trade-offs and assumptions\nChunking, embedding choice, retrieval mode, access filtering, and evaluation are workload-specific and require testing. This is standard RAG, not agentic retrieval, and no response-quality guarantee is implied. Model deployment choice, private networking, identity filtering, and content safety are explicit follow-up decisions.\n\nSource: [Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/rag/rag-solution-design-and-evaluation-guide).\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- Origin provider: Microsoft\n- Target: Azure conceptual adaptation\n- Primary source: [Design and Develop a RAG Solution on Azure](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/rag/rag-solution-design-and-evaluation-guide)\n- Reviewed: 2026-11-06\n\n## Catalog status\n\nThis is an original, editable Azure conceptual adaptation. It is synthetic reference material, not a deployed system, certified vendor architecture, or automatic deployment plan.\n\n## Mapping caveats\n\n- Index access must enforce the caller's document permissions; the source emphasizes evaluation, not automatic correctness.",
